.TH EUCLID-CLI-ETS-UPDATE-SERVER 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ets-update-server \- change an existing transfer server's settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ets update-server
.BR \-\-server\-id | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-port\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIPORT\fR]
[\fB\-\-bucket\fR|\fB\-b\fR \fIBUCKET\fR]
[\fB\-\-address\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIADDRESS\fR]
[\fB\-\-users\fR|\fB\-u\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-groups\fR|\fB\-g\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-host\-key\fR|\fB\-k\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-pasv\-min\fR \fIPORT\fR]
[\fB\-\-pasv\-max\fR \fIPORT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Changes an existing transfer server's settings. Only the options
actually given are altered, so one setting can be changed without
resending the whole definition. At least one option besides
.B \-\-server\-id
must be given.
.PP
.B \-\-users
and
.B \-\-groups
.I replace
the current list rather than adding to it. To add a user, pass the full
intended list; to close a server to everyone, note that removing both
lists entirely is not possible here - a server with neither cannot be
logged in to, and is better deleted.
.PP
A running server keeps running on its old settings: the manager restarts
a transfer server only when its desired state changes, not when its
definition does. Run
.BR euclid-cli-ets-stop-server (1)
followed by
.BR euclid-cli-ets-start-server (1)
to apply the change.
.PP
The protocol cannot be changed. FTP and SFTP are different processes
with different settings, so switching one for the other means deleting
the server and creating it again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-server\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the server to change. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " " \fIPORT\fR
TCP port to listen on.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fIBUCKET\fR
Name of the ESM bucket this server's clients read and write. Must
already exist. Objects already in the old bucket are not moved; clients
simply see the new bucket's contents after the server restarts.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-address " " \fIADDRESS\fR
Address to bind to.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-users " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated EAM user IDs allowed to log in. Replaces the current
list.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-groups " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated EAM user groups whose members may log in. Replaces the
current list.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-directories " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated directories every session should find under its home,
e.g. \fIincoming/mix,incoming/split,feedback\fR. Whichever of them do
not exist yet are created when a client logs in, relative to
.BR \-\-home\-dir ,
so under a \fI{user}\fR home the same list gives \fIjvo/incoming/mix/\fR
to one client and \fIoju/incoming/mix/\fR to another. Intermediate
levels are created too, since a directory is a marker object and a
client cannot walk into \fIincoming/mix\fR without \fIincoming\fR
existing in its own right. Existing directories are left untouched, so
this costs one listing per login and nothing after the first. Empty
creates nothing, which is what every server did before.
.PP
This exists because a client cannot be asked to create the shape it
delivers into \- it uploads to a folder that has to already be there \-
and doing it per login means adding a supplier to
.B \-\-users
is the whole of onboarding them.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-host\-key " " \fIPATH\fR
SFTP only. Private SSH host key file. Pointing a server at a different
key changes the identity clients see, and clients that pinned the old
one will refuse to connect until their known-hosts entry is updated.
.TP
.B \-\-pasv\-min " " \fIPORT\fR
FTP only. Lowest passive data port.
.TP
.B \-\-pasv\-max " " \fIPORT\fR
FTP only. Highest passive data port.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The server was updated; its full definition is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no option besides \fB\-\-server\-id\fR
given, the caller is not an authenticated administrator, the server
could not be reached, or the request was rejected - an unknown server
ID or bucket (HTTP 404).
.SH EXAMPLES
Open a server to an additional user group, then restart it so the change
takes effect:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets update-server \e
    --server-id sftp1 --groups transfer-users,auditors

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets stop-server \e
    --server-id sftp1
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets start-server \e
    --server-id sftp1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Point a server at a different bucket:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 ets update-server \e
    --server-id ftp1 --bucket archive
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-create-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-get-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-start-server (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ets-stop-server (1)
